{{Italic title|string=Basilio|noerror}}The '''Baliscan frigate Basilio''', is a three-masted wooden-hulled heavy [[W:Frigate|frigate]] of the [[Confederal Navy]]. She is the [[W:List of oldest surviving ships|oldest]] commissioned Baliscan naval warship still afloat. She was launched in 1814, one of [[Original eight frigates of the Confederal Navy|eight original frigates]] authorized for construction by the [[Confederal Congress]] and the fourth constructed. 

Designed to the young Bayaran Navy's capital ships, the ''Basilio'' and her sister ships were larger and more heavily armed and built than standard frigates of the period. She was built at [[Gundisalvus Malmudez]]'s shipyard in the North End of [[Macaio]], [[Bayara]]. Her first duties were to provide protection for Baliscan merchant shipping during the [[Continuation War]] with Spain and to defeat the [[Maroon pirates]] in the [[Maroon Wars]]. It is most famous for the occurrence of the [[Basilio mutiny|1827 Basilio mutiny]], in which the ship was taken over by it's rebelling crew in response to orders to enforce [[Maroon removal]] policy, sailed to [[Daramazán]] and defected to the [[Maroon Confederacy]]. The defection of the ''Basilio'' was a defining moment in the [[Confederal Question|escalation]] leading to the [[Baliscan Civil War]].

''Basilio'' was retired from active service following [[Araucaria]]'s confederation in [[1883 Confederal Convention|1883]] and was moored in [[Hisbanya]] to serve as a [[W:Receiving ship|receiving ship]]. She was designated a museum ship in 1927, on the 100th anniversary of the mutiny.

